name: Project quality checks

on:
  push:
  pull_request:
  workflow_dispatch:

permissions:
  contents: read

concurrency:
  group: quality-${{ github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: true

jobs:
  validate:
    name: Typecheck, production build and offline checks
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    timeout-minutes: 20
    steps:
      - name: Check out source
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Node.js
        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 22

      - name: Set up Python
        uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: '3.13'

      # Use the committed lockfile whenever it exists. The retry settings help
      # transient network failures; they do not bypass TLS or use a third-party mirror.
      - name: Install open-source dependencies
        shell: bash
        run: |
          npm config set fetch-retries 3
          npm config set fetch-retry-mintimeout 20000
          npm config set fetch-retry-maxtimeout 120000
          if [ -f package-lock.json ]; then
            npm ci --no-audit --no-fund
          else
            npm install --no-audit --no-fund
          fi

      - name: TypeScript typecheck
        run: npm run typecheck

      - name: Production build
        run: npm run build

      - name: Verify storefront foundation
        run: npm run verify:phase1

      - name: Verify database and security foundation
        run: npm run verify:phase2

      - name: Verify SQLite migration directly
        run: python scripts/verify-d1-schema.py

      # This workflow validates code only. It does not create Cloudflare resources,
      # modify production data, enable billing, or deploy the site.

# Build blocker diagnosis and resolution

## Root cause found

The earlier build was first blocked because the execution environment could not resolve `registry.npmjs.org` (`EAI_AGAIN`). That is an environment/network failure, not a compiler diagnostic.

A review against the official vinext App Router setup also found missing required dependencies in `package.json`:

- `react-server-dom-webpack`, the React Server Components runtime.
- `@vitejs/plugin-rsc`, the Vite React Server Components plugin.
- `@vitejs/plugin-react`, included in the official Vite/vinext setup dependencies.

The manifest now declares these packages and keeps `react`, `react-dom`, and `react-server-dom-webpack` on the same exact version (`19.3.0`) to avoid RSC version mismatch. `scripts/verify-phase2.mjs` now checks for these dependencies and version alignment.

The Wrangler configuration also had a concrete deployment defect: `assets` lacked its required `directory`. It now points to `./dist/client` (the vinext client build output) and sets `run_worker_first: true`, so the authentication/admin API requests are not short-circuited by static asset handling. The Phase 2 verifier now checks both settings.

## What has been done

- Added the missing App Router RSC dependencies.
- Added consistency checks so missing RSC dependencies are detected by the offline verifier.
- Added `.github/workflows/quality.yml` to install dependencies and run TypeScript checks, the full production build, the Phase 1/Phase 2 checks, and SQLite migration checks on GitHub-hosted runners.
- The workflow is validation-only; it does not deploy, create Cloudflare resources, or require Cloudflare secrets.

## What is and is not verified

Offline source and schema checks can be run without npm network access. The full build still cannot honestly be marked passed in the current container because npm cannot resolve `registry.npmjs.org` and the required packages therefore are not installed.

After pushing this commit to GitHub, inspect the **Project quality checks** workflow. It will verify dependency installation, semantic TypeScript checking, production build and local schema checks. If any step fails, use that actual log as the next defect report; do not assume the app is error-free just because syntax checks passed.

## Local reproduction

```bash
npm install --no-audit --no-fund
npm run typecheck
npm run build
npm run verify:phase1
npm run verify:phase2
python scripts/verify-d1-schema.py
```

If installation fails with `EAI_AGAIN`, restore DNS/network access to the official npm registry and rerun the commands. Do not bypass certificate validation or use untrusted package mirrors.

# GitHub Setup Guide — Harvinder Telecom E-Commerce

This guide is for applying the build-fix files to the **actual e-commerce repository**. Do not apply them to the separate `Harvinder Command` BI-dashboard project.

## Important status note

The project’s offline source checks and SQLite migration checks have passed in the prepared package. The full dependency install and production build have **not yet been confirmed** because the earlier environment could not resolve `registry.npmjs.org` (`EAI_AGAIN`). The GitHub Actions workflow added here will run the real install, TypeScript check, production build, and offline database checks after you push the branch. Do not call the project error-free until that workflow passes.

## Files included in the build-fix overlay

Copy these paths into the root of your existing e-commerce repository, preserving the directory structure:

- `package.json` — declares the Vite/vinext App Router dependencies with the React and React Server Components runtime versions aligned.
- `wrangler.jsonc` — contains the Cloudflare Worker, D1 and R2 bindings. `database_id` is still a placeholder and must not be used for a live deployment.
- `.github/workflows/quality.yml` — installs dependencies (preferring `npm ci` if a lockfile exists), then runs typecheck, production build and offline checks. It does not deploy.
- `docs/BUILD_BLOCKER.md` — records the build blocker and recovery steps.
- `docs/GITHUB_SETUP_GUIDE.md` — this guide.
- `docs/PULL_REQUEST_DESCRIPTION.md` — suggested PR title and description.

Do not copy the outer directory name inside the repository. Copy its **contents** into your repository root. If you have additional uncommitted work, back it up before replacing a file.

## Recommended method on Windows: GitHub Desktop

### Step 1 — Open the right repository

1. Open GitHub.com and navigate to your **Harvinder Telecom E-Commerce application source repository**.
2. Check the repository name and confirm its README describes the e-commerce storefront, product catalogue, database and inventory—not only the BI dashboard.
3. If the repository is not on your PC, click **Code → Open with GitHub Desktop** or clone it from GitHub Desktop using **File → Clone repository**.
4. In GitHub Desktop, use **Repository → Show in Explorer** to open the local repository folder.

### Step 2 — Create the fix branch before changing files

In GitHub Desktop:

1. Select **Current Branch**.
2. Select **New Branch**.
3. Enter `fix/build-and-ci`.
4. Create the branch from the current `main` branch (or your repository’s default branch).

Do not commit this fix directly to `main` unless you explicitly prefer that workflow.

### Step 3 — Download and extract the overlay

1. Download the file named `harvinder-telecom-ecommerce-github-fix-overlay.zip` from this conversation.
2. In Windows File Explorer, right-click the ZIP and choose **Extract All**.
3. Open the extracted overlay folder.
4. Copy the files and folders inside it into the root of your cloned repository. Keep folders such as `.github/workflows` and `docs` in the same relative paths.
5. If Windows does not show dot-folders, use File Explorer **View → Show → Hidden items**.
6. Replace only the same-named files listed in the overlay. Do not delete your other app files.

### Step 4 — Install and test locally

Install Node.js 22 (at least 22.12 for Vite 8 compatibility) and Python 3.13 or newer if not already available. Close and reopen your terminal after installation.

In the repository root, open PowerShell or the terminal built into GitHub Desktop and run:

```powershell
node --version
npm --version
python --version
npm install --no-audit --no-fund
npm run typecheck
npm run build
npm run verify:phase1
npm run verify:phase2
python scripts/verify-d1-schema.py
```

The package installation must complete first. When `npm install` succeeds, it creates `package-lock.json`. **Keep and commit that file** so dependency versions are reproducible; the GitHub workflow will then use `npm ci` automatically.

If `npm install` ends with `EAI_AGAIN`, `ENOTFOUND`, `ECONNRESET`, or a timeout, the registry/network was not reachable. Check your internet/DNS connection and retry the official npm registry. Do not disable TLS certificate validation or change to an unknown package mirror. A network error is not proof of a code error, but it also does not count as a successful build.

### Step 5 — Review changes in GitHub Desktop

1. Open the **Changes** tab.
2. Review every changed filename and diff.
3. Confirm no `.env`, `.dev.vars`, credentials, API keys, `node_modules`, or build output are being committed.
4. Confirm `package-lock.json` appears after a successful install.
5. Use this commit summary:

   `fix: resolve build dependencies and add automated quality checks`

6. Use this extended description:

   `Align React and React Server Components dependencies, document the build blocker, configure a CI workflow for typechecking/build/offline database checks, and keep deployment and paid billing disabled.`

7. Click **Commit to fix/build-and-ci**.

### Step 6 — Push the branch

Click **Push origin** in GitHub Desktop. This uploads the new branch to GitHub; it does not deploy the site.

### Step 7 — Open the pull request

1. Click **Create Pull Request** in GitHub Desktop, or open the repository on GitHub and select **Compare & pull request** for `fix/build-and-ci`.
2. Base: `main` (or the default branch). Compare: `fix/build-and-ci`.
3. Use the title:

   `fix: resolve build dependencies and add automated quality checks`

4. Copy the content from [`PULL_REQUEST_DESCRIPTION.md`](PULL_REQUEST_DESCRIPTION.md) into the description.
5. Create the pull request. Keep it open until the quality checks finish.

### Step 8 — Read the workflow result

1. Open the repository on GitHub.
2. Select **Actions**.
3. Open **Project quality checks** for the new branch or pull request.
4. Wait for these steps to complete:
   - Install open-source dependencies
   - TypeScript typecheck
   - Production build
   - Verify storefront foundation
   - Verify database and security foundation
   - Verify SQLite migration directly
5. If all steps are green, the checks in this workflow passed for that commit. If any step is red, open it, expand the failed step, and copy the actual error log before making another change.

A passing CI build still does **not** prove live D1, R2, real authentication, email, or deployment works. Those require separate tests with correctly configured resources. The workflow intentionally does not deploy or provision remote resources.

## Optional: GitHub website-only route

If you cannot use GitHub Desktop, you can edit text files one at a time using GitHub’s web editor, but uploading the overlay ZIP alone will not unpack it into your repository. In the repository, use **Add file → Create new file** for `.github/workflows/quality.yml` and the new docs, and edit the existing `package.json`, `wrangler.jsonc`, and `docs/BUILD_BLOCKER.md` using their **Edit** buttons. Preserve the existing source; do not replace the repository with only the ZIP's documentation. GitHub Desktop is safer for this multi-file update.

## What must remain unconfigured for now

- Do not run `npm run deploy` yet.
- Do not apply remote migrations yet.
- Do not replace the placeholder `database_id` with a guessed value.
- Do not commit `.dev.vars`, `.env`, Better Auth secrets or Cloudflare tokens.
- Do not enable paid services or automatic plan upgrades.
- Do not take real orders until database, authentication, image storage, stock reservation, payment handling, and recovery are tested end to end.

## If you need help debugging a failed run

Send the exact failed GitHub Actions step name and the full error excerpt around the first error. Do not send access tokens, passwords, `.env` contents, or secrets.

